---
/**
 * Vinyl vs fiberglass, drawn as a jamb cross-section rather than a swatch of
 * two colors. The real difference between the materials is wall structure —
 * hollow chambers vs solid pultrusion — and how much face width each one
 * needs to hold a given span, neither of which a photo of a finished window
 * can show. Labels live inside the drawing itself now, not just below it, so
 * the section reads on its own before anyone gets to the caption.
 */
interface Props {
  heading?: string | null;
}
const { heading = 'Vinyl and fiberglass, in section' } = Astro.props;
---
<figure class="mat-detail">
  {heading && <figcaption class="mat-heading">{heading}</figcaption>}

  <div class="mat-pair">
    <div class="mat-panel">
      <p class="mat-title">Vinyl</p>
      <p class="mat-sub">Multi-chamber, welded corners</p>

      <svg
        class="mat-svg"
        viewBox="0 0 240 190"
        role="img"
        aria-label="Cross-section of a vinyl window frame: three hollow chambers side by side inside a welded profile, next to the glass edge it holds."
      >
        <rect x="20" y="24" width="66" height="120" class="m-frame-vinyl" rx="3" />
        <rect x="27" y="31" width="15" height="106" class="m-chamber-cell" />
        <rect x="45" y="31" width="12" height="106" class="m-chamber-cell" />
        <rect x="60" y="31" width="12" height="106" class="m-chamber-cell" />
        <rect x="75" y="31" width="8" height="106" class="m-chamber-cell" />

        <!-- Welded corner: the miter line every vinyl frame actually has. -->
        <path d="M20 24 L32 36" class="m-weld" />
        <text x="34" y="18" class="m-tiny">Welded corner</text>

        <rect x="96" y="34" width="16" height="100" class="m-glass-edge" />
        <rect x="112" y="34" width="58" height="100" class="m-glass" />

        <!-- Face width bracket, aligned so the vinyl/fiberglass panels compare directly. -->
        <line x1="20" y1="160" x2="86" y2="160" class="m-dim" />
        <line x1="20" y1="155" x2="20" y2="165" class="m-dim" />
        <line x1="86" y1="155" x2="86" y2="165" class="m-dim" />
        <text x="53" y="176" class="m-dim-label">Frame face width</text>

        <text x="53" y="152" class="m-label">Frame</text>
        <text x="141" y="152" class="m-label">Glass</text>
      </svg>

      <ul class="mat-notes">
        <li><span class="swatch s-vinyl"></span>Hollow chambers trap air, add rigidity</li>
        <li>Lowest cost per opening; the Washington default</li>
        <li>Best on standard-size openings, most colors baked in</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="mat-panel">
      <p class="mat-title">Fiberglass</p>
      <p class="mat-sub">Solid pultruded profile</p>

      <svg
        class="mat-svg"
        viewBox="0 0 240 190"
        role="img"
        aria-label="Cross-section of a fiberglass window frame: a narrower, solid pultruded profile with internal reinforcing ribs, needing less face width than vinyl for the same opening."
      >
        <rect x="34" y="24" width="46" height="120" class="m-frame-glass" rx="2" />
        <rect x="34" y="24" width="46" height="120" class="m-frame-hatch" rx="2" />
        <line x1="48" y1="31" x2="48" y2="137" class="m-rib" />
        <line x1="64" y1="31" x2="64" y2="137" class="m-rib" />

        <rect x="96" y="34" width="16" height="100" class="m-glass-edge" />
        <rect x="112" y="34" width="58" height="100" class="m-glass" />

        <!-- Same bracket, shorter span — the visual point is that it's shorter. -->
        <line x1="34" y1="160" x2="80" y2="160" class="m-dim" />
        <line x1="34" y1="155" x2="34" y2="165" class="m-dim" />
        <line x1="80" y1="155" x2="80" y2="165" class="m-dim" />
        <text x="57" y="176" class="m-dim-label">Frame face width</text>

        <text x="57" y="152" class="m-label">Frame</text>
        <text x="141" y="152" class="m-label">Glass</text>
      </svg>

      <ul class="mat-notes">
        <li><span class="swatch s-glass"></span>Denser material, thinner sightline</li>
        <li>Holds shape over wide sliders and large pictures</li>
        <li>Costs more; paintable rather than color-molded</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p class="mat-foot">
    Both hold up in Washington weather. The choice is mostly about span: a wide slider or a tall
    picture window can flex a standard vinyl frame over time, where fiberglass holds its shape —
    which is also why it can get away with a narrower face for the same opening, shown above at
    the same scale. On a typical bedroom or bath opening, vinyl is the workhorse for a reason.
  </p>
</figure>

<style>
  .mat-detail {
    margin: 0;
  }

  .mat-heading {
    display: block;
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-m);
    font-size: var(--step-2);
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-display);
    line-height: var(--leading-display);
  }

  .mat-pair {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: var(--space-m);
  }

  .mat-panel {
    padding: var(--space-m);
    background: var(--paper-raised);
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
  }

  .mat-title {
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-3xs);
    font-size: var(--step-1);
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: -0.015em;
  }

  .mat-sub {
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-s);
    font-size: var(--step--1);
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }

  .mat-svg {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    background: var(--paper-sunk);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
  }

  .m-frame-vinyl {
    fill: color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 30%, transparent);
    stroke: var(--ink);
    stroke-width: 2.25;
  }

  .m-frame-glass {
    fill: color-mix(in srgb, var(--ink) 32%, transparent);
    stroke: var(--ink);
    stroke-width: 2.25;
  }

  /* A tight diagonal hatch reads as "solid, dense material" at a glance,
     contrasted against the open chamber cells on the vinyl panel. */
  .m-frame-hatch {
    fill: none;
    stroke: color-mix(in srgb, var(--ink) 45%, transparent);
    stroke-width: 3;
    stroke-dasharray: 0.001 6;
    stroke-linecap: round;
  }

  .m-chamber-cell {
    fill: color-mix(in srgb, var(--paper-raised) 70%, transparent);
    stroke: var(--ink-soft);
    stroke-width: 1.4;
    rx: 1.5;
  }

  .m-rib {
    stroke: color-mix(in srgb, var(--ink) 55%, transparent);
    stroke-width: 1.6;
  }

  .m-weld {
    fill: none;
    stroke: var(--accent-ink);
    stroke-width: 1.6;
    stroke-linecap: round;
  }

  .m-glass-edge {
    fill: color-mix(in srgb, var(--ink) 18%, transparent);
  }

  .m-glass {
    fill: color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 12%, transparent);
    stroke: var(--ink-soft);
    stroke-width: 1.3;
  }

  .m-dim {
    stroke: var(--ink-faint);
    stroke-width: 1;
  }

  .m-dim-label {
    fill: var(--ink-faint);
    font-family: Outfit, 'Outfit Fallback', system-ui, sans-serif;
    font-size: 7.5px;
    font-weight: 500;
    letter-spacing: 0.04em;
    text-anchor: middle;
  }

  .m-tiny {
    fill: var(--accent-ink);
    font-family: Outfit, 'Outfit Fallback', system-ui, sans-serif;
    font-size: 7.5px;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.02em;
  }

  .m-label {
    fill: var(--ink-soft);
    font-family: Outfit, 'Outfit Fallback', system-ui, sans-serif;
    font-size: 9px;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    text-anchor: middle;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  .mat-notes {
    margin: var(--space-s) 0 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-3xs);
    font-size: var(--step--1);
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }

  .mat-notes li {
    display: flex;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: 0.5rem;
  }

  .swatch {
    width: 0.7rem;
    height: 0.7rem;
    border-radius: 2px;
    flex: none;
    translate: 0 0.1rem;
  }

  .s-vinyl {
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 30%, transparent);
    border: 1.5px solid var(--ink);
  }

  .s-glass {
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--ink) 32%, transparent);
    border: 1.5px solid var(--ink);
  }

  .mat-foot {
    margin: var(--space-m) 0 0;
    max-width: var(--measure);
    font-size: var(--step--1);
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }

  @media (max-width: 800px) {
    .mat-pair {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
  }
</style>
